<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
    iOS press feedback.

    Android's stock `?android:attr/selectableItemBackground` is a ripple: an
    expanding circle that scales with the finger. iOS has no ripple at all —
    on iOS a control that is tappable dims to its "highlighted" fill the
    instant the touch goes down, and returns on release or cancel. Anything
    that keeps a ripple looks like Material the instant it is on screen, which
    is exactly what the replace-from-source strategy forbids.

    HIG → Color → Fill / systemFillTertiary is the fill iOS uses for a
    highlighted (pressed) row, so that is what this selector paints.

    Note this is a *selector*, not a ripple drawable, so it also costs nothing
    to draw: a StateListDrawable over two ColorDrawables is two fillRect calls
    on the GPU path, versus a RippleDrawable allocating a mask and running a
    shader per frame. That matters on the Helio G99 at 90 Hz.
-->
<selector xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">
    <item android:state_pressed="true">
        <color android:color="@color/fill_tertiary" />
    </item>
    <item android:state_focused="true">
        <color android:color="@color/fill_quaternary" />
    </item>
    <item android:state_activated="true">
        <color android:color="@color/fill_quaternary" />
    </item>
    <item>
        <color android:color="@android:color/transparent" />
    </item>
</selector>